WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:06.470
The convolution operation is one of the fundamental building blocks of a convolutional neural network.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:13.770
Using edge detection as the motivating example, in this video you see how the convolution operation works.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:23.530
In previous videos, I've talked about how the earlier layers of the neural network might detect edges,

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:27.030
and then the somewhat later layers might detect parts of objects,

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:33.530
and then even later layers maybe detect parts of complete objects like people's faces in this case.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:40.030
In this video, you see how you can detect edges in an image.

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:47.530
Let's take an example. Given a picture like that for a computer to figure out what are the objects in this picture,

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:53.530
the first thing you might do is maybe detect vertical edges in this image.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:59.330
For example, this image has all those vertical lines where the railings are,

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:03.830
as well as vertical lines that are outlined in these pedestrians.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:09.530
Those get detected in this vertical edge detector output.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.530
You might also want to detect horizontal edges.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:17.030
For example, there's a very strong horizontal line where this railing is,

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:21.530
and that also gets detected roughly here.

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:27.530
So how do you detect edges in an image like this? Let's look at an example.

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:32.830
Here is a 6x6 grayscale image, and because this is a grayscale image,

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:41.530
this is just a 6x6x1 matrix rather than 6x6x3 because they're on separate RGB channels.

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:45.330
In order to detect edges, or let's say vertical edges in this image,

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:50.830
what you can do is construct a 3x3 matrix, and in the parlance,

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:57.330
when the terminology of convolutional neural networks, this is going to be called a filter.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:02.830
I'm going to construct a 3x3 filter, or 3x3 matrix that looks like this.

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:08.330
1 1 1 0 0 0 minus 1 minus 1 minus 1.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:12.830
Sometimes research papers would call this a kernel instead of a filter,

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:17.330
but I'm going to use the filter terminology in these videos.

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:22.330
What you're going to do is take the 6x6 image and convolve it,

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:27.330
and the convolution operation is denoted by this asterisk,

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:32.090
and convolve it with the 3x3 filter.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:37.090
One slightly unfortunate thing about the notation is that in mathematics,

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:41.090
the asterisk is the standard symbol for convolution,

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:45.090
but in Python, this is also used to denote multiplication,

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.090
or maybe element-wise multiplication.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:52.590
This asterisk has dual purposes, it's overloaded notation,

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:58.590
but I'll try to be clear in these videos when this asterisk refers to convolution.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:04.590
The output of this convolution operator will be a 4x4 matrix,

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:08.590
which you can interpret, which you can think of as a 4x4 image.

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:12.590
When you compute this 4x4 output, it's as follows.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:18.090
To compute the first element, the upper left element of this 4x4 matrix,

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:26.090
what you're going to do is take the 3x3 filter and paste it on top of the 3x3 region of your original input image.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:34.590
I've written here 1 1 1 0 0 0 minus 1 minus 1 minus 1.

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:38.090
What you should do is take the element-wise product,

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:41.090
so the first one would be 3 times 1,

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:44.090
and then the second one would be 1 times 1,

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.090
I'm going down here, 1 times 1,

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:50.090
and then plus 2 times 1, this one,

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:53.090
and then add up all of the resulting 9 numbers.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:57.090
So then the middle column gives you 0 times 0,

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:00.090
plus 5 times 0, plus 7 times 0,

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:03.590
and then the rightmost column gives 1 times minus 1,

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:08.090
8 times minus 1, plus 2 times minus 1.

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:15.090
Adding up these 9 numbers will give you negative 5,

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:19.090
and so I'm going to fill in negative 5 over here.

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:22.090
You can add up these 9 numbers in any order, of course.

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:27.090
It's just that I went down the first column,

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.090
then the second column, then the third.

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:32.090
Next, to figure out what is this second element,

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:38.590
you're going to take the blue square and shift it one step to the right, like so.

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:41.590
And let me get rid of the green marks here.

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:45.590
And you're going to do the same element-wise product and then addition.

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:48.590
So you have 0 times 1,

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:50.590
plus 5 times 1,

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.590
plus 7 times 1,

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:55.590
plus 1 times 0, plus 8 times 0,

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:58.590
plus 2 times 0, plus 2 times negative 1,

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:00.090
plus 9 times negative 1,

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:02.090
plus 5 times negative 1.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:06.590
And if you add up those 9 numbers,

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:09.590
you end up with negative 4.

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:12.090
And so on. If you shift this to the right,

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:14.590
do the 9 products and add them up,

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:16.090
you get 0,

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:19.090
and then over here, you should get 8.

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:25.590
And just to verify, you have 2 plus 9 plus 5, that's 16,

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:28.590
then the middle column gives you 0,

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:30.590
and in the rightmost column,

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:35.090
4 plus 1 plus 3 times negative 1, that's minus 8.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:39.090
So that's 16 on the left column, minus 8,

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:43.090
and that gives you 8, like we have over here.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:47.090
Next, in order to get you this element,

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:50.090
in the next row, what you do is take the blue square

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:52.090
and now shift it one down,

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:54.090
so you now have it in that position.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:57.590
And again, repeat the element-wise product

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:01.090
and then adding exercise, and if you do that,

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:03.090
you should get negative 10 here,

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:06.560
and if you shift it one to the right,

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:10.560
you should get negative 2,

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:12.560
and then 2,

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:14.560
and then 3,

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.560
and so on,

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:20.560
to then fill in all the rest of the elements of the matrix.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:22.560
And so, to be clear,

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:25.560
this minus 16 would be obtained by,

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:30.060
you know, from this lower right 3 by 3 region.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:34.060
So a 6 by 6 matrix convolved with a 3 by 3 matrix

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:37.060
gives you a 4 by 4 matrix.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.060
And these are images and filters,

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:43.060
these are really just matrices of various dimensions.

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:47.060
But the matrix on the left is convenient to interpret

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:49.060
as an image,

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:51.060
and the one in the middle,

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.060
we interpret as a filter, and the one on the right,

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:56.560
we can interpret that as maybe another image.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:00.560
And this turns out to be a vertical edge detector,

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:03.060
and you see why on the next slide.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:05.560
Before going on, there was just one other comment,

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:09.560
which is that if you implement this in a programming language,

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.560
then in practice, most programming languages

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:13.560
will have some different function

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:16.560
rather than an asterisk to denote convolution.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.560
So for example, in the programming exercise,

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:22.560
you use or implement a function called

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.560
conv-forward, if you do this in TensorFlow,

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:33.560
there's a function tf.nn.conf2d,

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:36.560
and then other deep learning programming frameworks

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:38.560
in the Keras programming framework,

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:40.560
which you'll see later in this course,

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:42.560
there's a function called conv2d

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:45.560
that implements convolution, and so on.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:47.560
But all the deep learning frameworks

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:49.560
that have good support for computer vision

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:52.560
will have some function for implementing

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:54.560
this convolution operator.

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:59.450
So why is this doing vertical edge detection?

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:02.450
Let's look at another example.

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:05.450
So to illustrate this, we're going to use a simplified image.

00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:09.450
So here is a simple 6x6 image

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:12.450
where the left half of the image is 10

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:14.450
and the right half is 0.

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:16.450
If you plot this as a picture, it might look like this,

00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:19.450
where the left half, the 10s,

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:21.450
give you brighter pixel intensity values,

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:24.450
and the right half gives you darker pixel intensity values.

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:27.450
I'm using that shade of gray to denote zeros,

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:31.450
although maybe it could also be drawn as black.

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:35.450
But in this image, there's clearly a very strong vertical edge

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:37.450
right down the middle of this image

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:40.450
as it transitions from white to black

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:43.450
or white to a darker color.

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:48.450
So when you convolve this with the 3x3 filter,

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:52.450
and so this 3x3 filter can be visualized as follows,

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:57.450
where it is lighter, brighter pixels on the left,

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:59.450
and then these mid-tone zeros in the middle

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:01.450
and then darker on the right.

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:05.450
What you get is this matrix on the right.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:09.450
So just to verify this math that you want,

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:15.450
this 0, for example, is obtained by taking the element-wise products

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:18.450
and then multiplying with this 3x3 block,

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:23.450
and so you get from the left column 10 plus 10 plus 10,

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:26.450
and then zeros in the middle, and then minus 10 minus 10 minus 10,

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:30.450
which is why you end up with 0 over here.

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:36.450
Whereas in contrast, if that 30 will be obtained from this,

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:40.450
which you get from having 10 plus 10 plus 10,

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:43.450
and then minus 0 minus 0,

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:46.450
which is why you end up with a 30 over there.

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:49.450
Now if you plot this right-most matrix as an image,

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:51.450
it will look like that,

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:54.450
where there's this lighter region right in the middle,

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:59.450
and that corresponds to this having detected this vertical edge

00:09:59.450 --> 00:10:02.450
down the middle of your 6x6 image.

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:06.450
And in case the dimensions here seem a little bit wrong,

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:08.450
you know, that the detected edge seems really thick,

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:12.450
that's only because we're working with very small images.

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:15.450
In this example, and if you're using, say,

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:18.450
a 1000x1000 image rather than a 6x6 image,

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:23.450
then you find that this does a pretty good job

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:27.450
really detecting the edges, the vertical edges in your image.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:30.450
And in this example, this bright region in the middle

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:33.450
is just the output image's way of saying

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:36.450
that it looks like there's a strong vertical edge

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:38.450
right down the middle of the image.

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:41.450
And maybe one intuition to take away from vertical edge detection

00:10:41.450 --> 00:10:46.450
is that a vertical edge is a 3x3 region,

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:48.450
since we're using a 3x3 filter,

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:51.450
where there are bright pixels on the left,

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:54.450
and you don't care that much what's in the middle,

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:57.450
and dark pixels on the right.

00:10:57.450 --> 00:10:59.450
And the middle of the image is really,

00:10:59.450 --> 00:11:02.450
in the 6x6 image, is really where, you know,

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:05.450
there could be bright pixels on the left

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:07.450
and dark pixels on the right,

00:11:07.450 --> 00:11:11.450
and that's why it thinks there's a vertical edge over there.

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:14.450
And convolution operation gives you a convenient way

00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:19.450
to specify how to find these vertical edges in an image.

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:23.450
So you've now seen how the convolution operator works.

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:25.450
In the next video, you see how to take this

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:28.450
and use it as one of the basic building blocks

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:30.450
of a convolutional neural network.
